  "description": "Task: Bot Design: The bot must be manually controlled and capable of gripping various blocks or shapes. It should be able to insert them into corresponding grooves carved into a cubic block. The gripping mechanism should be precise and able to handle various shapes securely. Control Mechanism: The bot can be either wired or wireless. If wired, the cable should be long enough to ensure it remains slack and does not interfere with the bot’s movement. Wireless control options include a dual-frequency remote, Bluetooth, or Wi-Fi. Task Precision: The bot should be able to precisely align and place the blocks into their respective grooves, requiring accurate control of its movements and gripping mechanism. Flexibility and Range: For wireless bots, ensure the control mechanism provides a stable connection and sufficient range to operate effectively within the task area without any interference or lag. Rules and Regulations: Each team can have a maximum of 6 participants and a minimum of 2 A team may consist of students from different colleges. Teams have to make their robot. They can't use the other team's robot. Certificates of Excellence will be awarded to the top three teams. Judges/organizers' decision will be final.",
